You can have a list of options, then prompt the user to enter a number, then validate the input, and then take action based on the selection. Here's a simple example:
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Game {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.println("You enter a room. There's a door to the left and a door to the right.");
        System.out.println("1. Go left");
        System.out.println("2. Go right");
        System.out.print("Choose an option: ");
        int choice = scanner.nextInt();
        switch (choice) {
            case 1:
                System.out.println("You go left. There's a treasure!");
                break;
            case 2:
                System.out.println("You go right. There's a monster!");
                break;
            default:
                System.out.println("Invalid choice.");
        }
    }
}
This is a basic example, but you can expand it by using loops to handle invalid input, or by creating a class to represent dialogues with options and responses.
